1
00:00:00,020 --> 00:00:01,982
This is my voice, cloned.

2
00:00:02,703 --> 00:00:04,645
I never read this script aloud.

3
00:00:05,346 --> 00:00:08,810
A model did, and almost
everything it said was fine.

4
00:00:09,450 --> 00:00:13,675
But one spot was not, and a check
found it before my ears did.

5
00:00:14,555 --> 00:00:15,956
Hi, I'm Ian.

6
00:00:16,676 --> 00:00:18,997
That check is one piece of a pipeline,

7
00:00:19,598 --> 00:00:21,699
and this series takes the pipeline apart:

8
00:00:22,279 --> 00:00:24,120
how one person makes a finished,

9
00:00:24,721 --> 00:00:28,102
narrated explainer series,
one problem at a time.

10
00:00:28,743 --> 00:00:30,504
This first video is the map.

11
00:00:31,244 --> 00:00:32,585
It shows where you could end up,

12
00:00:33,165 --> 00:00:34,666
how the pipeline gets you there,

13
00:00:35,266 --> 00:00:36,347
and whether it is for you.

14
00:00:37,247 --> 00:00:40,789
The test is simple: what you
bring, and what you get.

15
00:00:41,410 --> 00:00:46,212
You bring a computer with a decent graphics
card, and something you want to explain.

16
00:00:46,933 --> 00:00:50,115
I name the smallest card I have
tested in the description.

17
00:00:50,795 --> 00:00:53,377
You get a real episode, made yourself,

18
00:00:53,957 --> 00:00:56,218
in your own voice and only yours,

19
00:00:56,799 --> 00:01:00,261
with a few cloud models doing
the parts a home card cannot.

20
00:01:01,001 --> 00:01:04,364
Turning that idea into an
episode is a chain of steps,

21
00:01:04,964 --> 00:01:08,067
and the first one happens
before any audio exists.

22
00:01:08,807 --> 00:01:10,709
You decide what the episode says:

23
00:01:11,309 --> 00:01:15,993
one problem per episode, with every
fact written down and sourced.

24
00:01:16,694 --> 00:01:20,757
That sounds slow, but it is the
cheapest place to find a mistake.

25
00:01:21,457 --> 00:01:24,060
A wrong sentence costs nothing on paper.

26
00:01:24,820 --> 00:01:29,204
Once it is narrated and
animated, fixing it costs a lot.

27
00:01:30,204 --> 00:01:33,226
Once the script is solid,
the voice comes next,

28
00:01:33,847 --> 00:01:36,909
because the narration sets the
timing for everything else.

29
00:01:37,750 --> 00:01:42,194
Every picture is built to fit the
audio, never the other way around.

30
00:01:42,834 --> 00:01:46,297
And because a voice model can
slip in ways your ears miss,

31
00:01:46,877 --> 00:01:49,820
the pipeline checks the audio
instead of trusting it.

32
00:01:50,580 --> 00:01:54,322
With the narration fixed, the
pictures can be built to fit it.

33
00:01:55,042 --> 00:01:59,145
A cloud model writes each animation
as a small piece of code,

34
00:01:59,745 --> 00:02:02,326
and each frame is drawn
from the time alone.

35
00:02:03,007 --> 00:02:05,848
So the same moment always
draws the same frame,

36
00:02:06,448 --> 00:02:09,270
and you can render a
frame twice and compare.

37
00:02:09,970 --> 00:02:13,952
That is how I caught a ghost: a
leftover copy of a moving shape,

38
00:02:14,533 --> 00:02:17,654
one frame wide, that I never
would have seen by eye.

39
00:02:18,514 --> 00:02:22,938
Catching it took a machine, and
that is how this whole setup works.

40
00:02:23,719 --> 00:02:26,961
Three different jobs, each
done by a different model.

41
00:02:27,702 --> 00:02:31,566
One speaks, in my voice,
on my own graphics card.

42
00:02:32,466 --> 00:02:35,809
One listens, and compares
what it hears to the script.

43
00:02:36,550 --> 00:02:38,492
A cloud model writes the pictures,

44
00:02:39,072 --> 00:02:42,455
and a cloud agent runs the
pipeline and checks the work,

45
00:02:43,036 --> 00:02:44,817
so that someone checks the checkers.

46
00:02:45,778 --> 00:02:47,221
If you want to make something real,

47
00:02:47,822 --> 00:02:51,208
alone, and you don't mind a
machine that argues with you,

48
00:02:51,789 --> 00:02:53,232
this series is for you.

49
00:02:53,933 --> 00:02:57,801
Each part takes one problem, so
start with the one you have.

50
00:02:58,701 --> 00:02:59,863
So the idea is simple.

51
00:03:00,484 --> 00:03:05,494
Plan before you record, let the voice
lead, and check what your ears miss.

52
00:03:06,295 --> 00:03:08,720
Next, I'll show you how that check works.
